assassin's creed valhalla is a fantastic  game that plays wonderfully  with its setting and time period but  there's a lot to 873  a.d britannia that's glossed over or  there's tiny anglo-centric details and  wrinkles in the world  that may not mean too much to those  players that were born beyond the  borders of these british isles  9th century england was a weird place so  i'm here to try and give you a little  background to the kingdoms you'll travel  through  and the island on which you'll settle so  if you fancy something that is half  history lesson half video game breakdown  then i've got just the thing for you  so why is 873 a.d england in four main  pieces with four rulers in assassin's  creed  well as rome withdrew from britain in  1410 ad  they left a fractured political  situation and as such the person with  the largest band of fighters was able to  hold the largest pieces of land  by 650 a.d seven micro kingdoms had been  claimed by their chieftains or y'alls  these were bernarcia diera lindsay east  anglia mercier wessex and  kent they were again consolidated and  conquered to become the four kingdoms  that make up england in assassin's creed  valhalla  northumbria murcia wessex and east  anglia  and the country would remain this way  until 9 27 a.d  feuds between kingdoms would continue  and as such this was a chaotic part of  history for england  and the invading and settling danes took  advantage of that to install their own  puppet kings onto the thrones of petty  kingdoms  an element that is reflected in the  story of protagonist aval  many of the rulers are represented in  assassin's creed valhalla as well  one of those being the big bad from the  original valhalla trailer  alfred the great was a king that had  inherited a reign from his brother  alfred the first who spent his entire  rule fighting the  viking waves of the great heathen army  and while alfred the great was  considered a learned and merciful man  and an all-round strong ruler it's  unsurprising that the game paints him in  more of a dour light  considering the story's need for  antagonists and the division between  the danes and anglo-saxons  nearly every assassin's creed game has  featured a variety of languages within  its story  and valhalla is no different the  historical languages that make an  appearance in this game represent the  type of people within the isle of  britain  the main three as seen in the game are  anglo-saxon old english  the west germanic language that came to  england alongside post-roman settlers in  the 5th century  old norse which was brought in by viking  invaders and contained a few  similarities to old english  as they both stemmed from the  proto-germanic mother language from  mainland europe  and of course not to be left out there  is also latin all three have some  representation within the in-game world  firstly within the locations that you  visit for example grant and bridgeshire  one of the earliest areas you'll visit  in the game  becomes modern day cambridgeshire the  name grantbridge comes from the nearby  river grant  and the word shire in old english  denotes an official charge or district  under a governor  and grew to mean shia or county  accurately reflecting the trading post  commanded by the in-game character soma  snottinghamshire don't laugh this is  serious history  was a name that didn't appear until 868  but  breaking the name down in old english  reveals the history of its name  snot was the name of an anglian  chieftain inga  meant the people of and ham meant  homestead  it may have grown into modern nottingham  but the name as it stands in  assassin's creed valhalla is simply the  homestead of the people  of snot nice a couple more quick ones  too modern-day sussex which is spelled  somewhat differently in assassin's creed  is seemingly made up of suth meaning  south and seax which was saxon  becoming land of the south saxons and  off the back of that  i'm sure you can guess essex and wessex  and oxenford which may not surprise you  to learn  is the forward of the oxen isn't  language fun  further north in the map is where you  start seeing influences from overseas  much of the wandering populace will be  speaking norse and of course one of the  most prominent cities in the game  jorvik which is modern day york holds on  to its scandinavian moniker ravens  thought the settlement which you build  up through your time in game  also retains a normic naming structure  the word thorpe in  old norse translates to a secondary  settlement  which if you've played more than a few  hours of the story you'll know that's  exactly what that small town is  latin however seems to be reserved for  the christian clergy in the game  you can hear them speaking it if you  blend in with their groups at certain  locations  like london ambulatory  a place which still holds much of the  ancient roaming naming conventions  such as the londonium amphitheater while  the titles used for some of the named  anglo-saxon enemies don't appear in  history until  sometime beyond the 9th century setting  of assassin's creed valhalla there are  however historical tidbits that reflect  your enemies and their combat style  yeomans for example were a social  standing above the common peasant or  foot soldier  but lower than knights as they were  commonly used as guards and were better  trained  this is reflected by their sturdier  shields and defensive posture in game  gedrick were the personal followers of a  chieftain and was sworn to die with him  as such they'd commonly get access to  better supplies such as helmets  and chainmail and were professional  soldiers by trade  again reflected both cosmetically and  existing as much tougher enemies than  the normal soldiers you face  and a banaret was a type of knight with  the right to lead a company of troops in  a time of war the in-game enemy wielding  a greatsword and covered in armor  goes some way to creating that nightly  vibe but when it comes to the leading of  men  they also have the special ability to  command nearby saxon archers  to fire a full volley at evil  the tradition of flighting seems to have  derived from the gaelic phillips social  class  of professional poets but the word  fighting actually comes from an  old english verb philtan meaning to  quarrel  but was performed as a ritualistic  exchange of insulting poetry from as  early  as the 5th century while you were  introduced to the pastime on the shores  of norway  it is not a completely nordic phenomenon  as you see through the towns of  assassin's creed that both dane and  saxon alike take part in the swapping of  hot disses  there is also literature documenting  nordic gods taking part in flighting  with loki in particular insulting the  other gods  in the anglo-saxon epic of beowulf the  titular character uses flighting as a  prelude to battle  or as combat in its own right and  shakespeare even wrote instances of  flighting  into many of his comedies one aspect of  fighting that assassin's creed plays  upon however is the jovial and often  good-natured intention of the verbal  back and forth performing well will  almost always get supportive responses  from whatever oath you just  linguistically put down  and historically in anglo-saxon england  the contest of wits would often take  place in a feasting hall with the winner  being decided by the reactions of the  crowd  and finished with the winner quaffing  enlarge mead and graciously inviting the  defeated party to drink along with them  as well  this has not only been faithfully  co-opted into a fantastic mini-game in  the world of valhalla's england  but also goes some way into showcasing  the importance of poetry  and intellect in medieval cultures this  goes hand in hand with hearing aval  performing their own intimate poetry in  cutscenes  and out in the world as well as crew in  your longboat using word and wit to  entertain the other vikings rowing  alongside them  there are also some lesser known and  unlived locations that are a big part of  british history  but may be unknown to those who hail  from further shores hadrian's wall for  example is a former defensive  fortification of roman britannia  built around 112 a.d during the reign of  emperor hadrian  an 80 mile long wall of varying height  and thickness it was built to guard the  wild northern border from the picts  a group of celtic speaking people who  lived in what is today  scotland as aval heads to the most  northern reaches of assassin's creed  valhalla's england  you may choose to visit some of both the  crumbling and still withstanding  sections of the wall  if you choose to journey across the  whole thing and like the brazilians as  you go  you'll be awarded with the achievement  is anyone out there and while historic  accounts of the purpose of the war vary  theories relate to the policy of defense  and hadrian's biographer wrote that the  hadrian was the first to build a wall  80 miles long to separate the romans  from the barbarians  indeed parts of this war still stand  today the larger sections being  preserved as part of the history of  britannia under the roman empire  although parts are also missing as  up to the 19th century farmers were  straight up stealing the stonework  to build their own farm walls another  more esoteric site can be found on the  hills of sussex as  aval roams the southern countryside the  long man of wilmington or the wilmington  giant  is in the real world a 235 foot tall  figure of a man holding two staves  its origins however remain unclear it  has previously been thought to originate  during the iron age or even neolithic  period  but 2003 archaeological investigation  may have shown  that it could have been formed during  the 16th or even 17th century  it is indeed one of britain's most  mysterious residents but a figure that  has been theorized to be a fertility  symbol or even an ancient warrior  is now a tourist attraction protected  cultural site  and a figure that a football team  vandalized and painted a giant knob on  back in 2010 and of course a site that's  likely familiar  to many stonehenge in valhalla it exists  both as a landmark and as one of the  game's standing stone puzzles  rewarding you with xp and a skill point  on its solution  unfortunately avor shed no real world  light as the true purpose to this  prehistoric  monument it remains however one of the  most famous landmarks of the united  kingdom  and a world heritage site it was built  by a culture that left  no written records so its purpose and  method of construction  are still debated and theorized maybe it  was those isu  alien folk from the game that made it  however one unfortunate note is that you  cannot  parkour over the real world version  while there are many more historical  tidbits hidden within aval's adventures  that's about all we've got time for  today  and i've yet to even find all of them  myself i hope you enjoy this weird mash  of history lesson and video game lore  exploration  if you did be sure to let us know in the  comments and tell us about your favorite  site in assassin's creed valhalla as  always like the video  and for more like this be sure to  subscribe to gamespot  you

